This is a fork. The changes carried here are stated:

  2026-09-26  DecodePartial reports what a truncated stream did decode.

              Decode is all-or-nothing: a file still arriving yields a nil
              image and "not enough pixel data" whatever fraction of it is
              present, which is the same as having nothing. Measured on a
              real 29 KB picture cut at four fractions, the standard decoder
              returned nothing every time. image/jpeg and image/gif answer
              the same way, so this is a gap in the shape of every decoder
              rather than in one of them.

              DecodePartial returns the image at its full declared size
              together with the number of pixel rows that are FINISHED,
              counting from the top.

              readImagePass already decodes row by row into the image and
              then discards it when io.ReadFull runs out of bytes. The
              change keeps it: at that point the rows above y are complete,
              y itself is the row whose bytes ran out, and claiming y would
              hand a caller a band of unfiltered noise. An INTERLACED image
              records nothing, because an Adam7 pass covers a subset of the
              whole frame and "rows from the top" describes nothing about
              it; DecodePartial then refuses it with a nil image.

              Decode's body moved into a decodeStream method so that
              DecodePartial runs the same decode rather than a second copy
              of the same loop. Decode, DecodeConfig and Encode behave
              exactly as before, and Go's own test suite passes here
              unaltered apart from the testdata path in the fuzz seed and
              the import path in the example.
